## Script requirements (scripts/analyze_impact.py)
Dependencies: pandas, numpy, scipy, matplotlib only (no statsmodels or lifelines; implement the methods directly).

CLI arguments:
- csv (positional)
- --start
- --end
- --cutoff (go-live date)
- --categories (comma-separated)
- --resolver
- --washout-days (default 14)
- --unit hours|bdays
- --asof (extract date for censoring open cases; default is the max end date)
- --min-n (default 20)
- --n-boot (default 5000)
- --n-boot-seg (default 2000)
- --out (output directory)

1. Prep and data quality:
   - Parse timestamps.
   - Compute duration (for open cases, measure to the as-of date and flag them as censored).
   - Count missing starts, duplicates, negative durations, washout exclusions, straddlers (opened pre, closed post), and open cases per period.
2. Macro:
   - Pre/post profile: n, median, p75, p90, mean, skew.
   - Bootstrap the DIFFERENCE (post − pre) for median, p90, and mean. Resample each group independently each iteration, vectorized in batches. Report the 95% percentile CI, a two-sided bootstrap p-value, and % change.
   - Ratio of geometric means via Welch t-test on log durations, with CI.
   - Kaplan–Meier median time-to-close per period, including censored cases.
   - Interrupted time series on weekly log-median (weeks with n≥5): OLS with intercept, pre-trend, level change, and slope change. Report betas, SEs, p-values, and multiplicative effects.
3. Micro:
   - For each category column and for the resolver column, compute per-level n_pre, n_post, median and p90 pre/post.
   - If both periods have n≥min-n, add: bootstrap median-difference CI, p-value, % change, and GM ratio.
   - Apply Benjamini–Hochberg q-values within each column.
   - Kitagawa mix-vs-rate decomposition of the change in mean for each category column.
   - Adjusted effect: OLS of log(duration) on post + category dummies + resolver fixed effects, restricted to resolvers present in both periods. Use cluster-robust (by resolver) sandwich SEs with small-sample adjustment, and df = clusters−1. Report exp(beta_post) with a 95% CI; this is the headline effect.
4. Outputs to --out:
   - summary.md with markdown tables of all of the above, including the count of significant segments at q<0.10 and resolver improvement stats (share improved, median and IQR of % changes).
   - CSVs for each table.
   - PNGs: pre/post histogram on a log x-axis, weekly median trend with a go-live line, and forest plots of % change in median with CIs for each category and for the resolver.

